<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siyavash</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Siyavush, Siavash</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siyavash is a legendary prince of Iranian tradition, son of King Kay Kavus, whose story is told in Ferdowsi's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>. Falsely accused by his stepmother Sudabeh, he proved his innocence by passing through fire, then fled to Turan, where he was unjustly killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a>, a death avenged by his son Kay Khosrow. According to al-Biruni the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> traced their line to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siyavash.jpg" alt="Siyavash" width="196" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siyavush_(The_Shahnama_of_Shah_Tahmasp).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its stories include the romance of Zal and Rudaba, the Seven Stages (or Labours) of Rostam, Rostam and Sohrab, <b>Siyavash</b> and Sudaba, Bijan and Manijeh, the wars with Afrasiab, and Rostam and Esfandyar.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings issued their own silver coinage, and later Khwarazmian tradition traced their line to the hero <b>Siyavush</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-khwarazm">Kingdom of Khwarazm</a></li></ul></section>
